---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import ClassificationSim from '../../simulators/classification/ClassificationSim.astro';
import { classificationQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/classification';

const toc = [
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why classify animals?' },
  { id: 'keys', label: 'Dichotomous keys' },
  { id: 'vertebrates', label: 'Vertebrate groups' },
  { id: 'invertebrates', label: 'Invertebrate groups' },
  { id: 'hierarchy', label: 'The levels of classification' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the key simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 27.2: Features Used to Classify Animals. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/27-2-features-used-to-classify-animals' },
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Chapter 29: Vertebrates. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/29-introduction' },
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Chapter 28: Invertebrates. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/28-introduction' },
  { text: 'Animal Diversity Web. University of Michigan Museum of Zoology.', url: 'https://animaldiversity.org/' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="zoology"
  topic="animal-classification"
  description="Interactive animal classification key: answer yes/no questions to sort 22 animals into mammals, birds, fish, reptiles, amphibians, insects, arachnids, crustaceans, molluscs, annelids, cnidarians and echinoderms."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={classificationQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Is a dolphin a fish? Is a spider an insect? Is a starfish a fish at all? Scientists sort living things into groups
      based on the features they share. Work through an identification key, question by question, and discover where
      some surprising animals really belong.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <ClassificationSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose an <strong>animal</strong> from the menu, or press <strong>Next animal</strong> for a random one.</li>
      <li>Answer each <strong>yes/no question</strong> about the animal. The key on the left highlights your path.</li>
      <li>If an answer is wrong, you get an explanation and can try again. Classify every animal without mistakes to raise your score.</li>
      <li>Stuck? Press <strong>Show me the answer</strong>. Click any box in the key to read about that feature or group.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset</strong> clears your score.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="why">Why classify animals?</h2>
  <p>
    Scientists have described well over a million animal species, and many more remain undiscovered. Classification
    organizes this diversity into groups so that we can identify organisms, communicate clearly about them, and
    understand how they are related. Modern classification aims to reflect <strong>evolutionary relationships</strong>:
    animals in the same group share a common ancestor and the features they inherited from it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="keys">Dichotomous keys</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>dichotomous key</strong> is a tool for identifying organisms. “Dichotomous” means dividing into two: each
    step asks a question with two possible answers, and each answer leads to another question or to a name. Good keys
    use clear, observable features — such as the number of legs or the type of body covering — rather than vague ones
    like size or colour, which can vary within a species.
  </p>

  <h2 id="vertebrates">Vertebrate groups</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Body covering</th><th>Breathing</th><th>Body temperature</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Fish</td><td>Scales</td><td>Gills</td><td>Cold-blooded (ectotherm)</td><td>Salmon, shark</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Amphibians</td><td>Moist skin</td><td>Gills when young; lungs and skin as adults</td><td>Cold-blooded</td><td>Frog, salamander</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reptiles</td><td>Dry scales</td><td>Lungs</td><td>Cold-blooded</td><td>Snake, sea turtle</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Birds</td><td>Feathers</td><td>Lungs</td><td>Warm-blooded (endotherm)</td><td>Eagle, penguin</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mammals</td><td>Hair or fur</td><td>Lungs</td><td>Warm-blooded</td><td>Dolphin, bat, platypus</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="invertebrates">Invertebrate groups</h2>
  <p>About 95% of animal species have no backbone. Some major groups:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Arthropods</strong> have jointed legs and an exoskeleton. They include <strong>insects</strong> (6 legs), <strong>arachnids</strong> (8 legs) and <strong>crustaceans</strong> (usually 10 legs, mostly aquatic). Centipedes and millipedes form a further group, myriapods.</li>
    <li><strong>Molluscs</strong> have soft bodies, often with a shell — snails, clams and the shell-less octopus.</li>
    <li><strong>Annelids</strong> are segmented worms such as earthworms and leeches.</li>
    <li><strong>Cnidarians</strong> such as jellyfish, corals and sea anemones have stinging cells.</li>
    <li><strong>Echinoderms</strong> such as starfish and sea urchins have spiny skin and five-part symmetry.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="hierarchy">The levels of classification</h2>
  <p>
    Carl Linnaeus, in the 18th century, organized life into nested levels and gave each species a two-part Latin name
    (binomial nomenclature). From broadest to most specific:
  </p>
  <code class="formula">Domain → Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species</code>
  <p>
    For example, the grey wolf is <em>Canis lupus</em>: kingdom Animalia, phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order
    Carnivora, family Canidae, genus <em>Canis</em>, species <em>lupus</em>. Pet dogs are usually classed as a
    subspecies, <em>Canis lupus familiaris</em>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Vertebrate / invertebrate</dt>
    <dd>An animal with / without a backbone.</dd>
    <dt>Exoskeleton</dt>
    <dd>A hard outer covering that supports and protects the body, as in insects and crabs.</dd>
    <dt>Endotherm / ectotherm</dt>
    <dd>An animal that keeps its body warm with its own heat (“warm-blooded”) / relies on its surroundings (“cold-blooded”).</dd>
    <dt>Species</dt>
    <dd>A group of organisms that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Field biology:</strong> ecologists use keys to identify species when surveying habitats.</li>
    <li><strong>Medicine and public health:</strong> correctly identifying mosquitoes, ticks or venomous animals matters for preventing disease and treating bites.</li>
    <li><strong>Agriculture:</strong> farmers need to tell pests from helpful insects, such as aphids from ladybirds.</li>
    <li><strong>Conservation:</strong> protecting endangered species starts with knowing exactly which species are present.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Whales and dolphins are fish.”</strong> They are mammals that breathe air.</li>
    <li><strong>“Spiders are insects.”</strong> They are arachnids with eight legs.</li>
    <li><strong>“Starfish and jellyfish are fish.”</strong> Neither has a backbone.</li>
    <li><strong>“All mammals give birth to live young.”</strong> The platypus and echidnas lay eggs.</li>
    <li><strong>“Bats are birds.”</strong> They are flying mammals with fur and skin-covered wings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the key simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The key covers 12 well-known groups and asks about typical features. Real classification includes many more groups
    (sponges, flatworms, myriapods and others) and is increasingly based on DNA comparisons. Some animals break simple
    rules — for example, some fish breathe air and some amphibians lack lungs — so scientists use many features together.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Animals are classified by shared features that reflect common ancestry.</li>
    <li>Dichotomous keys identify organisms through a series of two-choice questions.</li>
    <li>Vertebrates include fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals; most animals are invertebrates.</li>
    <li>Where an animal lives or how it moves is less important than its body features.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
